<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramakrishna Mission</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1897">1897</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramakrishna">Ramakrishna</a> Mission is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> religious and philanthropic organisation founded in 1897 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swami-vivekananda">Swami Vivekananda</a> and named after his teacher, the Bengali mystic Ramakrishna. Combining monastic life with social service, it runs schools, hospitals and relief work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and abroad, and teaches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a> as a universal philosophy. Its headquarters are at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belur">Belur</a> Math, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Kolkata</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramakrishna">Ramakrishna</a> (1836 to 1886) was a priest at the Dakshineswar Kali Temple who attracted monastic and household disciples. In 1886, shortly before his death, he gave ochre cloths of renunciation to young disciples planning to become renunciants and entrusted them to Vivekananda. After his death they formed the first monastery, or Math, at Baranagore and took informal monastic vows on the night of 24 December 1886.</p><p>Vivekananda became a wandering monk and in 1893 was a delegate at the Parliament of the World's Religions, where his speech opening with the words Sisters and Brothers of America brought him wide recognition. He founded the first Vedanta Society in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. Returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, he founded the Ramakrishna Mission on 1 May 1897. The same year Swami Akhandananda began famine relief at Sargachi, and Swami Brahmananda became the first president of the Order. After Vivekananda's death in 1902, Sarada Devi, the spiritual counterpart of Ramakrishna, acted as advisory head of the young organisation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Math and the Mission are the two organisations directing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramakrishna">Ramakrishna</a> movement. The Math, also called the Ramakrishna Order, is the monastic body concerned with spiritual development; the Mission is the humanitarian body that carries out medical, relief and educational work. The Mission was registered in 1909 under Act XXI of 1860, and the elected trustees of the Math also serve as its Governing Body. The Baranagar monastery moved to Alambazar in 1892, then to a garden house near the present site in 1898, and in January 1899 to the plot at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belur">Belur</a> acquired by Vivekananda.</p><p>The movement teaches a modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a> rooted in Advaita, with elements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaktism">Shakta</a> and Vaishnava traditions, structured around the four paths of jnana, bhakti, karma and raja yoga, and holds that all religions lead to the same goal. Its work covers hospitals, dispensaries, schools, a university, colleges, vocational centres, rural and tribal welfare and relief after famines, floods, earthquakes and cyclones. Its monks are its principal workers. It is non-sectarian and ignores <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> distinctions, and its ashramas hold satsang and arati and observe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> festivals along with Christmas Eve and the birthday of the Buddha. By March 2025 it had 263 centres, 200 of them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Mission states that its aims are spiritual and humanitarian and have no connection with politics, though it supported the movement for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> and some political revolutionaries later joined the Order. In 1980 it petitioned the courts to be declared a non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> minority religion under Article 30 of the Indian constitution. The Calcutta High Court accepted the plea, but the Supreme Court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> ruled against it in 1995, finding that the Mission had all the characteristics of a Hindu organisation. Its awards include the Gandhi Peace Prize in 1998.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ramakrishna Mission” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1898 Swami Vivekananda, arguing that one became a Brahmin by learning rather than by birth, gave the mantra to non-Brahmins in the <b>Ramakrishna Mission</b>, and Dayananda Saraswati, founder of the Arya Samaj, gave it a rendering of his own.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gayatri-mantra">Gayatri Mantra</a></li></ul></section>
